 that I want to add some analytics service. Usually I use Google Analytics, but I wanted to know if anyone is using something else and would recommend.\nHistorical sources (2020-08-19); present applicability is not corroborated. Starting a new personal/side project is a point at which a buyer who had defaulted to Google Analytics reconsiders and actively asks for alternatives.\nHistorical sources (2023-02-13); present applicability is not corroborated. Google's announcement that it was sunsetting the then-current Google Analytics generation forced at least one blog owner to take action and migrate to an open-source alternative.\nPublication date unknown; retrieval today does not establish current applicability. Getting the information needed to make decisions clearly, on a single page, rather than through multi-step report configuration.\nPublication date unknown; retrieval today does not establish current applicability. Wanting basic signals only: which pages get traffic and which external links/referrers drive clicks, without complexity.\nHistorical sources (2024-03-21); present applicability is not corroborated. Wanting simple basics like user counts and traffic sources rather than a highly technical tool.\nPublication date unknown; retrieval today does not establish current applicability. Wanting to see which of a blog's own posts are read the most, as a simple recurring need.\nHistorical sources (2021-08-10); present applicability is not corroborated. At least one developer cited widespread cookie/GDPR consent banners as part of his reasoning for abandoning cookie-based analytics, though the quote generalises to 'many websites' rather than confirming it was on his own site.\nPublication date unknown; retrieval today does not establish current applicability. blog.opencagedata.com hosts a company's own first-person post about switching its analytics tooling.\nPublication date unknown; retrieval today does not establish current applicability. news.ycombinator.com (Ask HN / tool-submission threads) is a place where this audience discusses and requests Google Analytics alternatives.\nHistorical sources (2024-03-21); present applicability is not corroborated. indiehackers.com hosts recurring threads where indie builders ask for Google Analytics substitutes.\nHistorical sources (2020-11-02, 2021-08-10, 2023-02-13); present applicability is not corroborated. dev.to hosts multiple individual developers' first-person posts about replacing Google Analytics on their own blogs/portfolios.\nReview required: this problem may reverse the cited source's meaning. Google Analytics (and, for at least one buyer, GA4 specifically) is reported as too complex or confusing to quickly answer simple traffic questions, pushing some site owners toward simpler alternatives.\nP2: Historical sources (2021-08-10, 2023-02-13); present applicability is not corroborated.\nP3: Historical sources (2021-08-10, 2023-02-13); present applicability is not corroborated.\nIgnored unavailable dossier ranking E239.",
